  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2020-06-16T18:37:08.051Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "Clicking Left Thumbstick to crouch for VR users.\nI would like to suggest that, for VR users, having it so that clicking on the left thumbstick cycle between crouching and crawling, and clicking the right thumbstick, usually reserved for jumping, undo the toggle state of crouch or crawl, if the user's avatar is in that state, while still maintaining its meaning as jump when the user's avatar is standing.\nI make this request especially because, while using seated play in a VR headset, it is sometimes extremely hard to interact with things on or near ground level.  It's difficult enough to report as a bug or problem in play.\nI specifically make this point, because, on the Oculus Quest, documentation indicates that clicking either thumbstick will cause the user's avatar to jump, which is not the case.  Only clicking the right thumbstick causes jump, clicking the left thumbstick has no response.  I don't know if other VR sets have different implementations; even if so, I'd suggest something like that be implemented to expand the possibilities of seated play.\nThe only problem I can see about that, is that you wouldn't be able to jump while crouched.  Having that be the problem, however, seems to me like a huge upgrade from the current implementation.\nFor reference, the documentation is at https://docs.vrchat.com/docs/touch.",
